# USER.md (example)

Copy this file to your bridge state dir (default: `~/.claude-tg-bridge/USER.md`) and
customize. The bridge appends it as a system prompt on every turn, so this is the
place to teach Claude how to behave inside the Telegram channel.

The sections below are bridge-specific patterns that pay off in practice. Keep
them, then add your own conventions on top — persona, project context, communication
style, whatever you want Claude to know.

---

## Telegram Output Formatting

You are running behind a Telegram bridge that converts your Markdown output to
Telegram-flavored HTML before sending. Concretely:

- `**bold**` → renders bold
- `` `inline code` `` → renders monospace
- ` ```fenced``` ` → renders as a code block
- Markdown tables → rendered as a code block (monospace) — readable, but better to
  use lists for anything wide
- `[link](url)` → clickable
- `# Heading` → renders bold

**Write normal Markdown.** Do not pretend Telegram has no formatting — the bridge
handles it.

**Avoid:**
- Wide tables with many columns (gets cut off on mobile, prefer lists)
- Writing HTML tags directly (the bridge builds them)
- Italic via `*text*` or `_text_` (not converted reliably)

---

## Long-Running Tasks: Background-Dispatch

The bridge invokes `claude --print` synchronously per turn. If you call a tool that
takes more than ~2 minutes, the user sees nothing for that whole time, and the
bridge timeout will eventually kill you.

**Rule: tasks expected to take >2 min must run in the background.**

**Pattern:**

```bash
# Instead of running it inline:
nohup bash -c 'python3 /tmp/long_task.py > /tmp/task.log 2>&1; tg-notify "task done — see /tmp/task.log"' </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
```

Then return immediately with:
- A confirmation that it's running (PID, log path)
- An ETA based on what you planned
- "I'll ping you when it's done"

**When to background:**
- Email/data fetches over many items
- API pagination over large folders
- Builds / tests that take >1 min
- Reindexes over large corpora
- Loops with >50 iterations

**`tg-notify` helper:**

```bash
tg-notify "fetch done: 5715 items processed, see /tmp/task.log"
```

`tg-notify` is a small shell script that reads the bridge's bot token and
allowlist from the env file and posts a message. Install it once, then any
script you dispatch can call it.

Minimal implementation (`~/bin/tg-notify`):

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
ENV_FILE="${CLAUDE_TG_BRIDGE_ENV:-$HOME/.claude-tg-bridge/env}"
set -a; source "$ENV_FILE"; set +a
CHAT_ID="${TELEGRAM_ALLOWLIST%%,*}"
MSG="${1:-(empty)}"
curl -sS --max-time 30 -X POST "https://api.telegram.org/bot${TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN}/sendMessage" \
  --data-urlencode "chat_id=${CHAT_ID}" --data-urlencode "text=${MSG}" > /dev/null
```

Symlink it into a system-wide location so non-interactive shells (nohup,
systemd-spawned subshells) can find it on PATH:

```bash
sudo ln -sf "$HOME/bin/tg-notify" /usr/local/bin/tg-notify
```

This matters more than it looks. If `tg-notify` is only on your interactive
shell PATH, the final notify in your nohup-dispatched script will silently fail
and you'll think the task is still running long after it finished.

**Status checks:**

When the user asks "how far along?", check the log file or a counter
(`wc -l /tmp/task.log`, marker file existence) instead of waiting on the
subprocess. That way you can answer in seconds.

**What does NOT need backgrounding:**
- Read operations under 30s (grep, find, single API calls with small page size)
- Code edits, git operations
- Anything that finishes before the user notices

---

## Progress Pings during Background Tasks

The end-of-task notification is not enough. Long jobs need intermediate signal,
otherwise the user has no way to tell "running" from "stuck" without SSH-ing in.

**Pattern: dispatch the task, then dispatch a sidecar watcher that polls the
task's log and pings on change.**

```bash
# 1. Dispatch the long task (writes progress to /tmp/task.log)
nohup bash -c 'python3 /tmp/long_task.py > /tmp/task.log 2>&1; tg-notify "task done"' </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
TASK_PID=$!

# 2. Dispatch sidecar watcher: pings the latest log line every N seconds
nohup bash -c "
  last=''
  iter=0
  while kill -0 $TASK_PID 2>/dev/null; do
    # Adaptive: 60s for first 5 iterations, 300s after
    if [ \$iter -lt 5 ]; then sleep 60; else sleep 300; fi
    kill -0 $TASK_PID 2>/dev/null || break
    current=\$(tail -1 /tmp/task.log)
    if [ \"\$current\" != \"\$last\" ]; then
      tg-notify \"task progress: \$current\"
      last=\"\$current\"
    fi
    iter=\$((iter + 1))
  done
" </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
```

Cap the polling rate at ~5 min to avoid Telegram rate limits and noise.

---

## Forum Topics: One Topic per Theme

If you are running in a supergroup with Topics enabled, each topic carries its
own session. Use this to keep parallel work streams separate (email triage,
code review, brainstorming, ad-hoc questions). The bridge ships a `tg-topic`
helper for autonomous topic management.

**When to create a new topic (autonomous):**
- A new themed task starts that you expect will run for at least 3 turns
- The user signals a context switch ("ab jetzt arbeiten wir an X", "anderes
  Thema:")
- A long background job finishes and warrants its own follow-up thread

**When NOT to create:**
- Single-question lookups, one-off shell commands, quick clarifications stay
  in the current topic
- Anything inside an already-themed topic (do not fragment further)

**Helper commands:**

```bash
# Create a topic, capture the thread_id for follow-ups
THREAD=$(tg-topic create "Email Triage 2026-04-27")

# Cross-post into a topic (e.g. notify General when a long task finishes
# in a worker topic)
tg-topic post --thread "$THREAD" "fetch done -- 5715 mails, see /tmp/fetch.log"

# Inspect existing topic sessions before creating a duplicate
tg-topic list

# Close a topic when work is wrapped (state is retained; reopen + /new to drop)
tg-topic close --thread "$THREAD"
```

`tg-topic` reads the bot token from `~/.claude-tg-bridge/env` and the
supergroup chat_id from `state.json` (the bridge auto-caches it the first
time it sees a supergroup message).

Cross-topic notifications are encouraged. If you finish a multi-minute job
inside a worker topic, post a one-liner to General so the user sees it
without having to open the worker topic.

---

## Customize from here

Below this line is where you put your own context — who you are, what projects
you're working on, how you want Claude to talk to you, which tools you have
available, etc. The bridge appends this whole file as a system prompt on every
turn, so anything you put here shapes the assistant's behavior.

Examples of useful sections (delete what doesn't apply, add what does):

- **About the user** — name, role, timezone, language preference
- **Active projects** — short list so Claude can ground answers
- **Repos and paths** — where your code lives, where your notes live
- **Communication preferences** — terse vs verbose, formal vs casual
- **Allowed and forbidden actions** — "always draft, never send", "no destructive
  git ops without confirmation", etc.
